CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to provide relief for damage and support for livelihood stabilization and psychological recovery for persons who have suffered physical, mental, or property damage due to the mega-wildfires that occurred from March 21, 2025 through March 30, 2025 in Uiseong-gun, Andong-si, Yeongdeok-gun, Yeongyang-gun, and Cheongsong-gun of Gyeongsangbuk-do, Sancheong-gun and Hadong-gun of Gyeongsangnam-do, and Ulju-gun of Ulsan Metropolitan City, thereby promoting the stabilization, recovery, and sustainable development of disaster-affected areas.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows:
  1. "Mega wildfires in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan" means wildfires that occurred from March 21, 2025 through March 30, 2025 in Uiseong-gun, Andong-si, Yeongdeok-gun, Yeongyang-gun, and Cheongsong-gun of Gyeongsangbuk-do, Sancheong-gun and Hadong-gun of Gyeongsangnam-do, and Ulju-gun of Ulsan Metropolitan City;
  2. "Disaster-affected area" means an area declared as a special disaster area pursuant to Article 60 of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety in connection with the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan;
  3. "Victim" means a person who has suffered physical, mental, or property damage due to the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, or Ulsan among those falling under any of the following items:
  a. A person who resided in the disaster-affected area at the time of the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, or Ulsan;
  b. A person who was operating a place of business, engaged in employment, or pursuing studies in the disaster-affected area at the time of the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan;
  c. A person who owned movable or immovable property located in the disaster-affected area at the time of the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan;
  d. Any other person who was staying in the disaster-affected area at the time of the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan or who is otherwise related to the disaster-affected area, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  4. "Regional reconstruction" means a comprehensive restoration and development process aimed at resolving structural issues, including improvement of the residential environment, restoration of local communities, maintenance of local population, and response to depopulation risks, beyond mere restoration of physical damage in disaster-affected areas of the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, or Ulsan, and establishing a foundation for long-term sustainable development;
  5. "Industrial complex" means an industrial complex as defined in subparagraph 8 of Article 2 of the Industrial Sites and Development Act;
  6. "Temporary housing facility" means accommodation facilities or other facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree that are provided to victims who have lost their residence or whose residence has become practically uninhabitable due to the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan;
  7. "Wildfire waste" means household waste, building debris, and other types of waste generated as a result of the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan;
  8. "Integrated multifunctional cluster district" means a district characterized by concentration of residential, commercial, and public service functions, transit-oriented development, and an urban structure with enhanced energy efficiency and climate resilience.

Article 3 (Responsibilities of the State and local governments) (1) The State and local governments (hereinafter referred to as the "State, etc.") shall formulate and implement comprehensive policies for stabilization, recovery, and sustainable development of victims and disaster-affected areas.
  (2) The State, etc. shall take necessary budgetary measures to support victims and disaster-affected areas.
  (3) The State, etc. shall establish institutional procedures to collect opinions from victims and disaster-affected areas and actively reflect such opinions in the process of formulating and implementing policies.

Article 4 (Relationships to other Acts) This Act shall prevail over other Acts with respect to support for damage related to the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan; provided, where the application of other Acts is more favorable to victims and disaster-affected areas, those Acts shall apply.

CHAPTER II COMMITTEE ON DAMAGE SUPPORT AND REGIONAL RECONSTRUCTION

Article 5 (Committee on Damage Support and Regional Reconstruction for Areas Affected by the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan) (1) The Committee on Damage Support and Regional Reconstruction for Areas Affected by the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan (hereinafter referred to as "the Committee") shall be established under the Prime Minister to deliberate on and resolve the following matters:
  1. Additional support measures related to the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan, beyond support or compensation provided under the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety and other Acts;
  2. Inspection of projects implemented under this Act and related support measures;
  3. Inspection of payment of subsidies under this Act and related matters;
  4. Other matters referred to the Committee by the chairperson as deemed necessary for the performance of duties under this Act.
  (2) A support organization shall be established under the Ministry of the Interior and Safety to verify facts, collect and review related materials, and provide other administrative support necessary for deliberation and resolution by the Committee.

Article 6 (Composition of the Committee) (1) The Committee shall consist of not more than 15 members including 1 chairperson, and members shall be appointed or commissioned by the Prime Minister from among the following persons. In such cases, the member specified in subparagraph 1 shall serve as an ex officio member:
  1. Seven high-ranking public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service of the Ministry of National Defense, the Ministry of Interior and Safety, the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Ministry of Health and Welfare, the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Ministry of SMEs and Startups, the Ministry of Planning and Budget, the Korea Forest Service, and the Korea Heritage Service;
  2. A person qualified as a lawyer with at least 5 years of practical experience in the relevant field;
  3. A person with substantial knowledge and experience in health, medical, or welfare sectors;
  4. A person with expertise necessary for recovery from damage and related assistance in the fields of agriculture, forestry, fisheries, and industry;
  5. A person recommended by a victims' organization under Article 11 who falls under subparagraph 3 or 4;
  6. A person recommended by the head of a local government in the disaster-affected area.
  (2) The chairperson shall be appointed by the Prime Minister from among the members.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (2), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Committee shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 7 (Fact-finding survey) (1) The Committee may investigate facts and others necessary for deliberating on and resolving the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 5(1).
  (2) Where the Committee deems it necessary for a fact-finding investigation under paragraph (1), it may request relevant administrative agencies, or individuals, enterprises, organizations, etc. to submit necessary materials or to provide cooperation.
  (3) Relevant administrative agencies, individuals, enterprises, organizations, etc. requested to submit materials or provide cooperation under paragraph (2) shall comply with such request unless there is a special reason not to do so.
  (4) Where deemed necessary, the Committee may hear opinions from interested persons, such as victims.

Article 8 (Assistance following deliberation and resolution by the Committee) (1) The State, etc. may provide assistance for matters deliberated on and resolved by the Committee under Article 5 for the restoration of damaged facilities, stabilization of livelihood of affected residents, and management stability of micro enterprises (referring to small enterprises defined in Article 2 of the Framework Act on Micro Enterprises; hereinafter the same shall apply) and small and medium enterprises (referring to small and medium enterprises defined in Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises; hereinafter the same shall apply).
  (2) Matters necessary for the assistance under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER III SUPPORT FOR VICTIMS

Article 9 (Principles of suppoprt) The State, etc. shall formulate support measures for relief for physical, mental, and property damage sustained by victims.

Article 10 (Guarantee of participation by victims) The State, etc. shall fully consider the opinions of victims and other relevant persons when formulating and implementing assistance measures for victims and disaster-affected areas.

Article 11 (Victims' organizations) (1) Victims numbering 10 or more form a victims' organization and report it to the Mayor/Do Governor having jurisdiction over the relevant disaster-affected area, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The victims' organization under paragraph (1) may submit opinions to the Committee concerning agendas for deliberation and resolution under Article 5(1) through the local government having jurisdiction over the relevant disaster-affected area.
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures, methods, etc. for reporting under paragraph (1) and the submission of opinions under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 12 (Restriction on overlapping support) Various forms of support under this Act may be provided to victims who have received or are receiving support of the same nature due to the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan as of the enforcement of this Act, excluding such portion of support.

Article 13 (Request for cooperation concerning victims' financial transactions) The State, etc. may request necessary cooperation from relevant public institutions, financial institutions, and other related agencies or organizations in order to alleviate the financial burden of victims who are experiencing economic hardship due to financial debt.

Article 14 (Support for joint farming organizations and smart agriculture) (1) The State, etc. may provide support to corporations or organizations that jointly manage agriculture (hereinafter referred to as "joint farming organizations") for the efficient restoration and sustainable operation of farmland, orchards, and other such areas within the disaster-affected area.
  (2) A joint farming organization shall satisfy all of the following requirements:
  1. It shall satisfy the requirements under Article 27-3(2)1 and 2 of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Business Entities;
  2. It shall satisfy the joint management area and the number of participating agricultural business entities prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) The State, etc. may provide the following support necessary for the operation of joint farming organizations:
  1. Support for providing equipment and facilities for joint cultivation;
  2. Support for the processing, distribution, and sales of jointly produced agricultural products;
  3. Support for the operation and management of joint farming organizations;
  4. Education and technical assistance related to joint farming;
  5. Support for the construction and operation of smart farms (referring to intelligent farms based on information and communications technology; hereinafter the same shall apply).
  (4) The State, etc. may provide the following support in order to promote smart agriculture within the disaster-affected areas (referring to smart agriculture as defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Smart Agriculture Promotion and Support Act; hereinafter the same shall apply):
  1. Assistance with facilities and equipment related to smart agriculture;
  2. Assistance with facilities and equipment for the new establishment of smart farms;
  3. Assistance with training professional personnel in smart agriculture.
  (5) Detailed matters concerning the standards, procedures, etc. for support for joint farming organizations and smart agriculture under paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 15 (Support for damage recovery of micro merchants and small and medium enterprises) (1) The State, etc. may provide support to micro merchants and small and medium enterprises in the disaster-affected areas for expenses necessary for the restoration of buildings and facilities of business establishments damaged by the mega-wildfires in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan.
  1. Disposal and restoration of the sites and business-place buildings, facilities, equipment, and materials;
  1. Disposal and restoration of sites and business-place buildings, facilities, equipment, and materials;
  2. Assistance for the restoration of machinery, equipment, and production, distribution, and processing facilities at business places;
  3. Demolition of damaged facilities.
  (3) Detailed matters concerning the scope of damage, criteria for assessing damage and determining support amounts, and the requirements, procedures, and manner of support under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 16 (Support for damage recovery of industrial complexes and factories) (1) The State, etc. may conduct the following projects for the recovery of damage to industrial complexes and factories in the disaster-affected areas:
  1. Restoration of infrastructure facilities defined in subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  2. Restoration and expansion of common-use facilities as defined in subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Act on the Improvement of Urban Areas and Residential Environments.
  (2) Matters necessary for the projects under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) In the course of damage recovery under paragraph (1), the State, etc. shall give priority consideration to measures to strengthen competitiveness through advancement, eco-friendliness, and digital transformation of industrial complexes and factories.

Article 17 (Support for damage recovery of agriculture, forestry, and fisheries) (1) The State, etc. shall provide assistance for expenses necessary for the restoration of facilities, equipment (including agricultural machinery), and crops in the agriculture, forestry, and fisheries sectors in disaster-affected areas.
  (2) Detailed matters concerning assistance for recovery expenses, including the scope of damage, criteria for assessing damage, and criteria for determining assistance amounts under paragraph (1), shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) The State, etc. may provide the following assistance to restore production bases and to secure income for farmers, foresters, and fishers in the disaster-affected areas:
  1. Restoration of farmlands;
  2. Restoration of agricultural and forestry facilities, agricultural crops, and forest crops;
  3. Restoration of livestock breeding facilities and restocking of livestock, etc.;
  4. Restoration of fishing vessels, fishing nets, and fishing gear;
  5. Restoration of aquaculture facilities and propagation of aquatic products and stocking of aquatic organisms;
  6. Assistance for the restoration of forestry management bases, such as the removal of fire-damaged trees.
  (4) Notwithstanding Article 4 of the Act on the Prevention of and Countermeasures against Agricultural and Fishery Disasters, the State, etc. may expand the eligibility and scope of assistance for disaster recovery costs for farmers and fishers in disaster-affected areas.
  (5) The State, etc. may provide assistance to persons engaged in forestry in disaster-affected areas for expenses for the preparation of forest management plans pursuant to Article 13 of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act.
  (6) When selecting projects for forest product production complexes and forest composite management complexes pursuant to Articles 8 and 9 of the Forestry and Mountain Villages Development Promotion Act, the State, etc. may give priority to persons engaged in forestry who have suffered damage from the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan.

Article 18 (Financial support for tourism business operators) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may provide special loans (including deferment of repayment) to alleviate the management burden of tourism businesses (referring to tourism businesses registered under Article 4 of the Tourism Promotion Act) that have suffered direct or indirect damage due to the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan.

Article 19 (Special cases on the Emergency Aid and Support Act and the Care Support Act) (1) Persons prescribed by Presidential Decree Among the victims shall be deemed persons eligible for emergency support under Article 5 of the Emergency Aid and Support Act.
  (2) Where a gap in child care for the children of victims arises due to activities related to damage recovery, the head of a Si, Gun, or Gu (referring to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) may give priority to providing such victims with child care services as defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Child Care Support Act.
  (3) Matters necessary for the standards, period, methods, and procedures of support under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 20 (Support for psychological counseling) (1) The State, etc. shall provide necessary assistance, such as psychological counseling and daily life counseling, in order to ensure the psychological stability and social adaptation of victims.
  (2) Matters necessary for the content and methods of support pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 21 (Medical support) (1) The State, etc. shall provide necessary medical services to restore the physical and mental health of victims.
  (2) Matters necessary for the content and methods of medical service support pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 22 (Recovery of subsidies) (1) Where a person who has received monetary payments or other assistance under this Act falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the State, etc. shall recover an amount equivalent to the amount received within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree:
  1. Where such payments or assistance were received by false or other fraudulent means;
  2. Where such payments or assistance were made or provided by mistake, etc.
  (2) Where assistance funds are recovered pursuant to paragraph (1), if the person obliged to return such funds fails to do so by the prescribed period, they shall be collected in the same manner as delinquent national or local taxes are collected.

CHAPTER IV RESTORATION OF DISASTER-AFFECTED AREAS AND SUPPORT FOR REGIONAL RECONSTRUCTION

Article 23 (Support for economic reconstruction and revitalization of disaster-affected areas) The State may devise and implement special support measures to promote economic reconstruction and revitalization of disaster-affected areas.

Article 24 (Priority support for statutory policy projects) (1) The State, etc. may give priority support to the following projects in the disaster-affected areas:
  1. Forestry projects pursuant to Article 22 of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act;
  2. Aquaculture start-up projects pursuant to Article 65 of the Aquaculture Industry Development Act;
  3. Fishing village and fishery harbor regeneration project pursuant to Article 47-6 of the Fishing Villages and Fishery Harbors Act;
  4. Projects implemented in accordance with the disaster recovery plan pursuant to Article 46 of the Countermeasures against Natural Disasters Act;
  5. Large-scale disaster recovery projects and district-level comprehensive recovery projects pursuant to Article 49-2 of the Countermeasures against Natural Disasters Act;
  6. Recovery projects included in the disaster recovery plan pursuant to Article 59 of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety;
  7. Other projects prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) In providing priority support under paragraph (1), the following measures may be applied:
  1. Granting priority in project selection;
  2. Allocation of separate project quotas.

Article 25 (Special cases concerning priority allocation of Local Population Decline Response Fund for disaster-affected areas) (1) In allocating the Local Population Decline Response Fund pursuant to Article 25 of the Framework Act on the Management of Local Government Funds, the disaster-affected areas shall be given priority allocation by applying weighted values to the allocation criteria.
  (2) The Local Population Decline Response Fund allocated to disaster-affected areas shall be used on a priority basis for projects aimed at regional reconstruction and revitalization.
  (3) The special provisions on priority allocation of the Local Population Decline Response Fund for the disaster-affected areas shall apply until 2031; where necessary, they may be extended following deliberation by the State Council.
  (4) Detailed matters concerning the weighting of fund allocation, allocation ratios, and the scope of use under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 26 (Restoration and utilization of forests in disaster-affected areas) (1) The Ulsan Metropolitan City Mayor, the Gyeongsangbuk-do Province Governor, and the Gyeongsangnam-do Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor") shall formulate and implement execution plans for the restoration and utilization of forests in the disaster-affected areas. In such cases, the execution plans may include the following matters:
  1. Measures for restoring forest ecosystems and securing biodiversity;
  2. Measures for establishing systems for the cyclical use of forest resources;
  3. Measures for revitalizing the regional economy through the utilization of forests;
  4. Measures for strengthening forest disaster prevention and response systems;
  5. Measures for developing smart forestry, tourism, and leisure facilities based on fourth industrial technologies;
  6. Measures for utilizing forest resources to increase local residents' income;
  7. Measures for supporting the creation and utilization of carbon sinks, environmental, social, and governance (ESG)-based forestry projects, and bioresource production sites;
  8. Measures for hosting forest leisure sports competitions and establishing related facilities;
  9. Other measures for forest utilization suitable for regional characteristics.
  (2) The Mayor/Do Governor shall prepare measures to guarantee the substantial participation and profit-sharing of local residents in the process of establishing and implementing the action plan under paragraph (1).

Article 27 (Support for forest management in disaster-affected areas) (1) The State, etc. may designate and support special forest management zones in order to increase income through the utilization of forests in the disaster-affected areas.
  (2) Any of the following persons may apply to the State, etc. for designation of a special forest management zone under paragraph (1):
  1. Producers‘ organization as defined in subparagraph 4 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Agriculture and Fisheries, Rural Community, and Food Industry;
  2. Cooperative management and administration organizations at the village or regional level;
  3. Other forms of cooperative management organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) The State, etc. may provide the following assistance necessary for the operation of special forest management zones:
  1. Assistance for afforestation projects involving income-generating tree species suited to regional characteristics;
  2. Assistance for forest management equipment and facilities;
  3. Assistance for forest management projects;
  4. Assistance for the processing, distribution, and sale of forest products;
  5. Assistance for the operation and management of cooperative management organizations;
  6. Assistance for education and technology related to cooperative management.
  (4) Where a special forest management zone is designated, assistance may also be provided for income sources from other forest products produced in forests, excluding agricultural products, notwithstanding Article 8(2) of the Forestry and Mountain Villages Development Promotion Act.
  (5) Where a person specified in each subparagraph of paragraph (2) intends to use national forests designated as special forest management zones for the purpose of cultivating fruit-bearing trees among income-source forest products, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service may lease or permit the use of such national forests, notwithstanding Article 21(1)7 of the State Forest Administration and Management Act, to the extent that it does not hinder the management and administration of national forests.
  (6) Matters necessary for the requirements, criteria for assistance, procedures, etc. concerning special forest management zones under paragraphs (1) through (5) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 28 (Support for energy supply) (1) The State, etc. may give priority support for the following matters to improve energy-use efficiency and promote energy welfare for victims and residents living in the disaster-affected areas:
  1. Energy-related national support projects;
  2. New and renewable energy distribution project;
  3. Energy support and energy conservation projects for vulnerable groups;
  4. Living energy support projects, including the construction of liquefied petroleum gas pipelines;
  5. Other energy supply support as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The State, etc. may provide the necessary budgetary support for energy supply under paragraph (1).
  (3) Detailed matters concerning the targets, scope, criteria, and procedures of special cases concerning supply for energy supply under each subparagraph of paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 29 (Support for disposal of wildfire waste) (1) The State shall provide assistance for expenses incurred in the disposal of wildfire wastes generated by the mega-wildfires in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan and in the restoration of waste treatment facilities and equipment managed and operated by local governments in disaster-affected areas.
  (2) Detailed guidelines and procedures for supporting restoration of waste treatment facilities, equipment, etc. under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 30 (Support for hazardous tree removal projects) (1) Notwithstanding Article 22 of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act, where hazardous tree removal projects prescribed by Presidential Decree are carried out in the areas affected by the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan, the State, etc. may implement such projects at its discretion without obtaining the consent of the forest owners.
  (2) Where a hazardous tree removal project is implemented pursuant to paragraph (1), the forest owner shall be notified thereof immediately after the implementation of the project. In such cases, the consent of the forest owner shall be deemed to have been obtained.
  (3) If immediate notification under paragraph (2) is not feasible because the forest owner is absent or cannot be identified, the following procedures may be followed. In such cases, if the following procedures are followed, the consent of the forest owner shall be deemed obtained:
  1. Notification of the implementation of the project to the forest owner's last known address;
  2. If notification is impossible, posting a public announcement on the bulletin board and the website of the relevant local government for at least 30 days.
  (4) If the forest owner suffers a loss due to the implementation of a hazardous tree removal project under paragraph (1), such loss may be compensated.
  (5) Matters necessary for the procedures, methods, and standards for compensation, etc. of hazardous tree removal projects under paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 31 (Priority support for forestry income projects) (1) Notwithstanding Articles 4 and 8 of the Forestry and Mountain Villages Development Promotion Act, loans or subsidy projects in the field of forest income may be preferentially provided to the disaster-affected areas.
  (2) The State may also provide living expenses to members of households that collect forest products, such as pine mushrooms, Neungi mushrooms, and sap, pursuant to Article 66 (1) of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety.
  (3) Notwithstanding Articles 7, 8, 13, and 14 of the Act on the Operation of Direct Payment Program for Promoting Public Functions of Forestry and Forests, the forest land and recipients eligible for payment who have suffered damage from the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan shall continue to receive public direct payment for forestry and forests if they meet the requirements for eligibility prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Detailed matters concerning the standards and procedures for support under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 32 (Delegation of authority and special cases concerning disaster-affected areas) (1) For the prompt stabilization and recovery of disaster-affected areas in relation to the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan, the authority of the heads of the relevant administrative agencies under each of the following subparagraphs shall be vested in the Mayor/Governor:
  1. The authority of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service concerning the alteration or release of conservation forest land pursuant to Article 6 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act (limited to forest investment leading zones under Article 41);
  2. Authority of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service regarding permission for the conversion of mountainous districts pursuant to Article 14 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act (excluding national forests under the jurisdiction of the Korea Forest Service);
  3. Authority of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service concerning permission for temporary use of mountainous districts pursuant to Article 15-2 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act (excluding national forests under the jurisdiction of the Korea Forest Service);
  4. Authority of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service concerning the designation or release of natural recreation forests pursuant to Articles 13 and 19 of the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act (excluding national forests under the jurisdiction of the Korea Forest Service).
  (2) Where an implementer of a strategic forest investment project under Article 45(1) conducts land readjustment for the implementation of such project, the provisions on land readjustment under Articles 28 through 32, 32-2, 32-3, 33 through 36, 36-2, and 37 through 49 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis.
  (3) Where a person with authority to establish a strategic plan as defined in Article 2(1)4 of the Special Act on Promotion of and Support for Urban Regeneration changes a special regeneration plan pursuant to Article 36 of that Act, such person may, notwithstanding paragraph (2) of that Article, simplify consultations with the heads of relevant administrative agencies and deliberation procedures by local committees and obtain approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (4) The special cases under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall apply for 5 years from the enforcement date of this Act, and if necessary, they may be extended within a period not exceeding 3 years following deliberation by the State Council.
  (5) Matters necessary for the application of the special cases under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 33 (Special cases concerning application of standards for establishment of natural recreation forests) (1) In order to ensure efficient recovery of disaster-affected areas and revitalization of the local economy, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor shall apply the standards for creation area and feasibility assessment of natural recreation forests, etc. with relaxed requirements pursuant to Article 21-2 of the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act.
  (2) The standards for establishment area and feasibility evaluation of natural recreation forests, etc. under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 34 (Development and implementation of community recovery programs) (1) The State, etc. shall develop and implement programs for the psychological stability and community recovery of victims and residents of disaster-affected areas, taking into account the following:
  1. Characteristics such as nationality, race, religion, gender, age, and occupation of victims and residents of disaster-affected areas;
  2. Prevention of departure of victims and residents from the local community, and improvement of their quality of life;
  3. Promotion of distinctive local culture and strengthening community bonds in disaster-affected areas;
  4. Participation and linkage of non-profit private organizations, public interest organizations, and social clubs related to health, welfare, education, culture, and physical education located in disaster-affected areas.
  (2) Where the disaster-affected areas implement programs under paragraph (1), the State, etc. may provide all or part of the necessary expenses. In such cases, matters necessary for the content, methods, and period of such support shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) Where the State, etc. develop programs under paragraph (1), they may sufficiently collect opinions from the victims and residents of disaster-affected areas.
  (4) The State, etc. may conduct necessary survey, research, and others for the development and implementation of programs under paragraph (1).

Article 35 (Establishment of community complex facilities) (1) Where necessary for community recovery, a local government in the disaster-affected area may establish and operate complex facilities that provide services such as psychological counseling and health, welfare, care, education, labor, and cultural services, in consultation with the State.
  (2) The head of a complex facility referred to in paragraph (1) shall, if necessary to support the effective recovery of the community, have employees under his or her jurisdiction visit the families, etc. of victims to provide psychological counseling and services such as those related to health, welfare, care, education, labor, culture, etc. under paragraph (1).
  (2) The head of a complex facility referred to in paragraph (1) shall, if necessary to support the effective recovery of the community, have employees under his or her jurisdiction visit the families, etc. of victims to provide psychological counseling and services such as those related to health, welfare, care, education, labor, culture, etc. under paragraph (1).
  (4) Matters necessary for the establishment and operation of complex facilities under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the competent local government.

Article 36 (Establishment of multifunctional cluster districts) (1) The Mayor/Do Governor may create a multi-functional cluster district for the systematic recovery and sustainable regional reconstruction of the areas damaged by the mega-wildfires in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan.
  (2) The Mayor/Do Governor may formulate a master plan including the following matters to create multi-functional cluster districts:
  1. Re-layout of areas damaged by the mega-wildfires in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan and the designation of high-density development zones;
  2. Measures for the integrated development of transportation, energy, and environmental infrastructure based on living zones;
  3. Measures to ensure accessibility and residential conditions for the elderly and disaster-vulnerable groups;
  4. Standards for climate-crisis-responsive architecture and urban design.
  (3) The State, etc. may provide administrative and financial support necessary for the construction of infrastructure and the building of housing and living convenience facilities required for the creation of multi- functional cluster districts under paragraph (1).
  (4) Matters necessary for the implementation of paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the competent local government.

Article 37 (Reduction or exemption of charges) Where the State, etc. installs public facilities such as temporary housing facilities for disaster victims in disaster-affected areas, the following charges may be reduced or exempted in accordance with the relevant Acts:
  1. Development charges under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Restitution of Development Gains Act;
  2. Infrastructure installation costs under subparagraph 20 of Article 2 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  3. Farmland conservation charges referred to in Article 38(1) of the Farmland Act;
  4. Metropolitan transportation facility charges under Article 11(1) of the Special Act on the Management of Intercity Transport in Metropolitan Areas;
  5. Traffic inducement charges as defined in subparagraph 9 of Article 2 of the Urban Traffic Improvement Promotion Act;
  6. Substitute forest resources development charges under Article 19(1) of the Mountainous Districts Management Act;
  7. Ecosystem conservation charges under Article 46(1) of the Natural Environment Conservation Act;
  8. Substitute pastureland creation charges under Article 23(8) of the Grassland Act.

Article 38 (Support for manufacturing innovation for reconstruction of disaster-affected areas) (1) The Minister of SMEs and Startups and the heads of local governments of disaster-affected areas may expand and preferentially support smart factory dissemination projects under the Act on the Promotion of Smart Manufacturing Innovation for Small and Medium Enterprises in order to enhance the competitiveness of small and medium enterprises damaged by the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan.
  (2) Detailed matters concerning the scope, procedures, etc. of support under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 39 (Development of tourism complexes) (1) In order to promote economic recovery and sustainable development of disaster-affected areas, the State, etc. may ease and apply the requirements necessary for designation of tourism complexes under Article 52 of the Tourism Promotion Act, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Local governments may, in the development and operation of tourism complexes under paragraph (1), give priority to participation by and employment of residents of disaster-affected areas.

Article 40 (Implementation of memorial projects) (1) The State, etc. may implement the following projects to commemorate the victims of the mega-wildfires in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan and to raise awareness of disaster response; provided, the State may support local governments where such projects are implemented by local governments:
  1. Creation of memorial parks;
  2. Erection of memorial monuments;
  3. Construction of memorial halls;
  4. Collection, preservation, management, exhibition, and research of materials for memorial hall;
  5. Installation and operation of disaster evacuation facilities;
  6. Other related projects that are consistent with the objectives of the projects under subparagraphs 1 through 5.
  (2) The State, etc. may solicit public opinions through public contests or other means regarding the names of memorial parks, memorial monuments, and memorial halls under subparagraphs 1 through 3 of paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "memorial facilities").
  (3) The locations of memorial facilities shall be determined by the State, etc. within the disaster-affected areas, reflecting the opinions of local residents and bereaved families.
  (4) The State, etc. may install memorial facilities, notwithstanding the National Land Planning and Utilization Act, the School Health Act, the Act on Special Measures for Designation and Management of Development Restriction Zones, or other relevant Acts.

CHAPTER 5 DESIGNATION OF STRATEGIC FOREST INVESTMENT ZONES

Article 41 (Designation of strategic forest investment zones) (1) A Mayor/Do Governor may designate an area meeting the following requirements as strategic forest investment zone (hereinafter referred to as a "strategic zone") in order to promote sustainable development of disaster-affected areas and to accelerate private investment necessary therefor:
  1. The area must be at least 10,000 square meters and be suitable for the designated purpose, use plan, and surrounding conditions;
  2. The area shall serve the public interest by contributing to regional development, such as revitalizing the local economy and developing underdeveloped areas;
  3. The project shall be implementable in an environmentally and ecologically sustainable manner;
  4. There shall be no concern of causing disasters such as landslides or soil runoff;
  5. The investment plan for the strategic forest investment project shall be feasible;
  6. The ratio of mountainous districts damaged by the mega-wildfires in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan relative to the total area of the proposed project site shall be at least the ratio prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  7. Other requirements prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the competent City/Do shall be satisfied.
  (2) Where a Mayor/Do Governor intends to designate a strategic zone, they shall establish a development plan for the strategic zone under Article 43, consult with the heads of relevant administrative agencies (including the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service; the same shall apply in paragraph (3)), including consultation on strategic environmental impact assessment under Article 16 of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act, and undergo deliberation by the Strategic Forest Investment Zone Deliberation Committee under Article 42(1).
  (3) When a Mayor/Do Governor designates a strategic zone, they shall publicly notify the details thereof in the official bulletin as prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the City/Do, and notify the heads of relevant administrative agencies. In such cases, the public notice of topographic maps shall be governed by Article 8 of the Framework Act on the Regulation of Land Use.
  (4) When a Mayor/Do Governor designates and publicly notifies a strategic zone pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (3), they shall make the relevant documents available for public inspection for at least 14 days.
  (5) Where a Mayor/Do Governor intends to change the location, boundaries, or area of the strategic zone, paragraphs (2) through (4) shall apply; provided, this shall not apply to changes of minor matters prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the City/Do.
  (6) The State, etc. shall minimize haphazard development in the strategic zone, ensure harmony and balance between the conservation and use of mountainous districts and forests, and prepare necessary measures to achieve the objectives of designating a strategic zone.
  (7) A Mayor/Do Governor may, if necessary to investigate whether a project is being implemented in accordance with the details of the public notice under paragraph (3), request a strategic forest investment project operator designated under Article 45 to submit relevant data.
  (8) A Mayor/Do Governor may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, government-funded research institutes, or government-invested institutions to provide data, statistics, etc. necessary for analyzing the efficient management of strategic zones and their impact on the local economy.
  (9) If a strategic zone fails to meet the requirements under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), the Mayor/Do Governor may cancel the designation following deliberation by the Strategic Forest Investment Zone Deliberation Committee under Article 42.
  (10) Matters necessary for the procedures, methods, and management of the designation, change, or cancellation of strategic zones shall be prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the City/Do.

Article 42 (Forestry Investment Strategic Zone Deliberation Committee) (1) The Strategic Forest Investment Zone Deliberation Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Strategic Zone Deliberation Committee") may be established under the jurisdiction of the competent Mayor/Do Governor to deliberate on matters concerning the designation, change, and cancellation of the strategic zones.
  (2) The Strategic Zone Deliberation Committee shall consist of not more than 15 members, including one chairperson and two vice chairpersons, and members shall be classified as ex officio members and appointed members.
  (3) The Mayor/Do Governor shall serve as the chairperson of the Strategic Zone Deliberation Committee, and the vice chairpersons shall be a vice mayor or vice governor designated by the Mayor/Do Governor and one person elected from among the members of the Strategic Zone Deliberation Committee.
  (4) The ex officio members of the Strategic Zone Deliberation Committee shall be public officials belonging to the Senior Civil Service of the Korea Forest Service, and the commissioned members shall be appointed or commissioned by the competent Mayor/Do Governor, as prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the competent City/Do.
  (5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for the organization and operation, etc. of the Strategic Zone Deliberation Committee shall be prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the competent City/Do.

Article 43 (Forestry investment strategic zone development plan) (1) The Mayor/Do Governor shall formulate a development plan for the strategic zone (hereinafter referred to as the "strategic zone development plan").
  (2) The strategic zone development plan shall include the following matters:
  1. Name, location, and area of the strategic zone;
  2. Implementation method and period of the strategic forest investment project;
  3. Designation purpose and development direction of the strategic zone;
  4. Plans concerning land use and traffic treatment;
  5. Plans for the installation of major infrastructure, including roads, water supply and sewerage systems, and electric power facilities (including plans for bearing the costs thereof);
  6. Plans for ecology, landscape, and environmental conservation, and plans for preventing pollution;
  7. Safety and disaster countermeasures, and rescue and emergency medical plans;
  8. Plans for bearing the costs of trunk facilities, etc. installed in areas outside the strategic zone;
  9. Matters concerning the use, supply, and disposal of developed land, etc.;
  10. A compensation plan (including relocation measures);
  11. A detailed list of land, etc. subject to expropriation or use;
  12. Plans for financing and annual investment;
  13. Other matters prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of City/Do.
  (3) Where a Mayor/Do Governor formulates a strategic zone development plan, he or she shall, in advance, hear the opinions of the heads of competent Si/Gun/Gu, local residents, and experts, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 44 (Effects of designation of strategic zones) (1) When a strategic zone is designated and publicly notified, any matters previously consulted upon with the heads of relevant administrative agencies regarding the designation, establishment, change, or determination in the following subparagraphs shall be deemed to have been designated, established, changed, or determined on the date of such public notice, in accordance with the details publicly notified under Article 41(3):
  1. Designation of urban development zones pursuant to Article 3 of the Urban Development Act and establishment of urban development project plans pursuant to Article 4 of that Act;
  2. Designation of tourist destinations and tourist complexes pursuant to Article 52 of the Tourism Promotion Act;
  3. Formulation and amendment of a basic plan for public waters reclamation under Articles 22 and 27 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act;
  4. Determination of a river zone under Article 10 of the River Act and amendment of a river master plan under Article 25 of that Act (limited to local rivers under the River Act).
  (2) Except as provided in paragraph (1), Articles 24 through 26 of the Framework Act on Administration shall apply mutatis mutandis to the standards and effects of deemed designation, etc.

Article 45 (Designation of implementers of strategic forest investment projects) (1) The Mayor/Do Governor shall designate a project implementer of a strategic forest investment project (hereinafter referred to as "project implementer"; hereinafter the same shall apply) from among the following persons in accordance with the standards and procedures prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the competent City/Do:
  1. Local governments;
  2. Public institutions as prescribed by Presidential Decree among the public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
  3. Local public corporations established under the Local Public Enterprise Act;
  4. Private investors who meet the qualification requirements, such as capital, prescribed by the Municipal Ordinance of the competent City/Do;
  5. A corporation established by investment contributed by two or more of the persons under subparagraphs 1 through 4 for the purpose of implementing a strategic forest investment project, which meets the requirements prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of City/Do;
  6. An association established by landowners within the strategic zone for the purpose of implementing a strategic forest investment project.
  (2) In cases of designating a business operator pursuant to paragraph (1), the Mayor/Do Governor shall consider the following matters:
  1. Financial soundness and funding capacity;
  2. Experience in implementing development projects similar to the strategic forest investment projects;
  3. Other matters publicly notified by the Mayor/Do Governor as deemed necessary for the smooth implementation of strategic forest investment projects.
  (3) Where the Mayor/Do Governor designates a project implementer pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall publicly notify the details thereof in an official gazette.

Article 46 (Revocation of designation of project implementers and substitute designation) (1) Where a project implementer falls under any of the following subparagraphs, a Mayor/Do Governor may revoke the designation of such project implementer; provided, where the project implementer falls under subparagraph 1, the designation shall be revoked:
  1. In case of obtaining designation or approval under this Act through false or other improper means;
  2. Where construction or a project has commenced within 1 year after obtaining approval of an implementation plan under Article 47(1);
  3. Where approval of an implementation plan under Article 47(1) has been revoked;
  4. Where an application for approval of an implementation plan has not been filed by the deadline for application under Article 47(5);
  5. Where it is deemed difficult to achieve the purpose of a strategic forest investment project due to natural disasters, bankruptcy of the project implementer, or other grounds prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the City/Do.
  (2) Where a Mayor/Do Governor revokes the designation of a project implementer pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall publicly notify such revocation.
  (3) Where a Mayor/Do Governor intends to revoke the designation pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall hold a hearing.
  (4) Where a Mayor/Do Governor revokes the designation of a project implementer pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she may designate a new project implementer as a substitute. In such cases, Article 45 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the substitute designation of a project implementer.
  (5) A project implementer designated as a substitute pursuant to paragraph (4) shall succeed to the status of the former project implementer with respect to approval of the implementation plan.

Article 47 (Establishment and approval of implementation plan) (1) A project implementer shall prepare an implementation plan and obtain approval from the Mayor/Do Governor, as prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of City/Do. The same shall apply where the project implementer intends to change the approved implementation plan (excluding minor changes prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of City/Do).
  (2) An implementation plan shall include the following:
  1. Name, location, and area of the strategic forest investment project;
  2. Purpose and direction of implementation of the strategic forest investment project;
  3. Plan for the acquisition and use of land;
  4. Matters concerning district unit plans prepared pursuant to Article 52 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  5. Planned floor maps and summary design documents;
  6. A phased development plan (limited to cases where it is necessary to implement the development project in phases due to project conditions);
  7. A detailed list of land, etc. subject to expropriation or use;
  8. Other matters prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of City/Do.
  (3) Where the Mayor/Do Governor intends to approve an implementation plan or approve any modifications thereof, he or she shall consult with the heads of relevant administrative agencies and hear the opinions of residents as prescribed by the Municipal Ordinance of the City/Do.
  (4) When a Mayor/Do Governor has approved an implementation plan or any modifications thereof, he or she shall give public notice thereof in the Official Gazette as prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the competent City/Do, and forward copies of related documents to the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu. In such cases, the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu who has received copies of related documents shall make them available to the general public for at least 14 days.
  (5) An application for approval under the former part of paragraph (1) shall be filed within 2 years from the date on which the applicant is designated as a project implementer.

Article 48 (Deemed authorization and permission and relaxation of regulation) (1) Where the Mayor/Do Governor grants approval of an implementation plan or approval for modification thereof pursuant to Article 47 (1), matters that were consulted on with the heads of relevant administrative agencies under paragraph (3) with respect to the approval, permission, authorization, designation, determination, report, consultation, cancellation, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "authorization, permission, etc.") specified in the following subparagraphs shall be deemed granted the authorization, permission, etc. concerned; and where an approval of an implementation plan or an approval for modification thereof is publicly notified, public notice or public announcement of the relevant authorization, permission, etc. shall be deemed given:
  1. Permission for installation of discharge facilities under Article 11 of the Act on the Management and Use of Livestock Excreta, and permission for and reporting on modification thereof;
  2. Building permits under Article 11 of the Building Act, building notifications under Article 14 of that Act, modifications of permitted or notified matters under Article 16 of that Act, building permits for or notifications of temporary buildings under Article 20 of that Act, and building consultations under Article 29 of that Act;
  3. Permission for aggregate extraction under Article 22 of the Aggregate Extraction Act;
  4. Approval of business plans pursuant to Article 15 of the Tourism Promotion Act and approval of tourist destination and tourism complex development plans pursuant to Article 54 of that Act;
  5. Determination of an urban/Gun management plan under Article 30 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act (limited to facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree among plans under subparagraph 4d of Article 2 thereof, and designation of a district unit planning zone and a district unit plan under Article 51 thereof among plans under item e of that subparagraph); approval of topographic drawings under Article 32(2) of that Article; designation of a planned management area among non-urban areas under Article 36 of that Act (limited to cases subject to deliberation by an urban planning committee under Article 30(3) thereof); designation of a development promotion district under Article 37 thereof (limited to cases subject to deliberation by an urban planning committee under Article 30(3) thereof); permission for development activities under Article 56 thereof; designation of an implementer of an urban/Gun planning facility project under Article 86 thereof; and authorization of an implementation plan under Article 88 thereof;
  6. Designation of a rural tourism resort complex pursuant to Article 82(1) of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act;
  7. Modification or revocation of an agricultural promotion area under Article 31 of the Farmland Act, permission for, or consultation on, farmland conversion under Article 34 of that Act, reports on farmland conversion under Article 35 of that Act, permission for or consultation on temporary use of farmland for other purposes under Article 36 of that Act, and approval of change of use under Article 40 of that Act;
  8. Permission for, or report on, installation of discharge facilities under Article 23 of the Water Environment Conservation Act, Article 33 of the Water Environment Conservation Act, and Article 8 of the Noise and Vibration Control Act;
  9. Permission for road construction by a person other than the road management authorities under Article 36 of the Road Act, permission for road occupation under Article 61 of that Act, and consultation or approval with the road management authorities under Article 107 of that Act;
  10. Authorization of the project implementation plan pursuant to Article 50 of the Act on the Improvement of Urban Areas and Residential Environments;
  11. Permission for felling, etc. within land for erosion control under Article 14(1) of the Erosion Control Work Act; and revocation of designation of the land for erosion control under Article 20 of the Act;
  12. Permission for or report on acts within a forest protection zone (excluding forest genetic resource protection zones) under Article 9(1) and Article 9(2)1 and 2 of the Forest Protection Act; and revocation of designation of a forest protection zone under Article 11(1)1 of the Act (limited to forest protection zones damaged by the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan, excluding national forests under the jurisdiction of the Korea Forest Service);
  13. Permission for and reporting on felling of standing trees, etc. pursuant to Article 36(1) and (5) of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act;
  14. Approval of an implementation plan for development of a national industrial complex under Article 17 of the Industrial Sites and Development Act; approval of an implementation plan for development of a general industrial complex under Article 18 of the Act; and approval of an implementation plan for development of an urban high-tech industrial complex under Article 18-2 of the Act;
  15. Permission for or reporting on conversion of forest land under Articles 14 and 15 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act; permission for or reporting on temporary use of forest land under Article 15-2 of the Act; and permission for extraction of earth and rocks under Article 25 of the Act;
  16. Formulation and approval of comprehensive plans for small river maintenance and formulation of implementation plans for small river maintenance under Articles 6 and 8 of the Small River Maintenance Act; permission for maintenance of small rivers, etc. under Article 10(1) of that Act; and permission for or reporting on the occupation of small rivers, etc. under Article 14 of that Act;
  17. Authorization of general waterworks business and industrial waterworks business under Articles 17 and 49 of the Water Supply and Waterworks Installation Act, and authorization of the installation of private-use waterworks and private-use industrial waterworks under Articles 52 and 54 of that Act;
  18. Modification of designation and revocation of forestry promotion zones under Article 20 of the Forestry and Mountain Villages Development Promotion Act;
  19. Approval of business plan under Article 15 of the Housing Act;
  20. Approval of business plan under Article 12 of the Act on the Installation and Utilization of Sports Facilities Act;
  21. Permission for changes in land shape, etc. under Article 21-2 of the Grassland Act and permission for conversion of grassland under Article 23 of that Act;
  22. Approval or report of installation of waste treatment facilities under Article 29 of the Wastes Control Act;
  23. Authorization for the installation of public sewerage (limited to excreta treatment facilities) under Article 11 of the Sewerage Act; permission for public sewerage business under Article 16 of that Act; permission for the occupation of public sewerage under Article 24 of that Act; and reporting on the installation of private sewerage treatment facilities under Article 34(2) of that Act;
  24. Consultation with or approval from a river management agency under Article 6 of the River Act; permission for the execution of river works and authorization of implementation plans for river works under Article 30 of that Act; permission for the occupation of rivers, etc. under Article 33 of that Act; and permission for the use of river water under Article 50 of that Act.
  (2) The State, etc. may relax regulations on overlapping regulatory areas such as wetland protection areas pursuant to Article 8 of the Wetland Conservation Act, within the scope specified in the following subparagraphs:
  1. Restrictions on actions in wetland protection areas pursuant to Article 13 of the Wetland Conservation Act;
  2. Other relaxation of regulation prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) Where the Mayor/Do Governor intends to receive deemed authorization, permission, etc. under paragraph (1), and an implementation plan includes matters specified in any subparagraph of paragraph (1), he or she shall submit relevant documents prescribed by the applicable Acts in advance to the heads of relevant administrative agencies and consult therewith. In such cases, the head of the relevant administrative agency who has received a request for consultation shall submit his or her opinion within 30 days from the date of receipt thereof; if no opinion is submitted within such period, consultation shall be deemed completed.
  (4) Where authorization, permission, etc. under other Acts are deemed to have been granted pursuant to paragraph (1), any license tax or fees, etc. imposed under the relevant Acts shall be exempted.
  (5) The deemed authorization, permission, etc. and relaxation of regulation under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall apply for 5 years from the enforcement date of this Act; provided, such period may be extended within 3 years upon deliberation by the State Council.
  (6) Detailed standards for relaxation of regulation referred to in paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 49 (Inspection of completion) (1) Where a project implementer completes all or part of a strategic forest investment project (limited to cases where an implementation plan including a phased development plan under Article 47(2)6 has been approved), he or she shall prepare a project completion report and receive a completion inspection conducted by the Mayor/Do Governor in accordance with Municipal Ordinance of City/Do.
  (2) Upon receipt of a project completion report under paragraph (1), the Mayor/Do Governor shall conduct a completion inspection without delay. In such cases, where necessary for efficient inspection, he or she may request relevant administrative agencies, public institutions, research institutes, and other specialized institutions to conduct the completion inspection on his or her behalf.
  (3) The Mayor/Do Governor may request the head of a State agency, local government, or public institution that is to take over or manage public facilities included in the project completion report to participate in the completion inspection, and the person so requested shall comply therewith unless there is a special reason not to do so.
  (4) Where, as a result of the completion inspection under paragraph (2), the Mayor/Do Governor recognizes that the strategic forest investment project has been completed, he or she shall issue a certificate of completion inspection to the project implementer and publicly announce the completion of the project; where the project has not been completed in accordance with the implementation plan, he or she shall, without delay, order to take necessary measures such as supplementary construction.
  (5) Where the Mayor/Do Governor conducts a completion inspection under paragraph (2), matters consulted upon with the heads of relevant administrative agencies pursuant to paragraph (7) regarding completion inspection, completion authorization, etc. under authorization, permission, etc. deemed granted pursuant to Article 48 shall be deemed to have undergone such completion inspection or completion authorization, etc.
  (6) In order for a project implementer (excluding cases where the project implementer is the Mayor/Do Governor) to receive deemed completion inspection or completion authorization, etc. under paragraph (5), he or she shall submit the relevant documents prescribed by the applicable Acts along with an application for completion inspection under paragraph (1).
  (7) Where, in conducting a completion inspection under paragraph (2), matters falling under completion inspection, completion authorization, etc. under authorization, permission, etc. deemed granted pursuant to Article 48 are included, the Mayor/Do Governor shall consult in advance with the heads of relevant administrative agencies.
  (8) No created land or installed facilities shall be used before a completion inspection under paragraph (1) is conducted.

Article 50 (Support for installation of infrastructure) (1) The State, etc. may directly build infrastructure defined in subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act or subsidize expenses incurred in building such infrastructure to ensure the smooth implementation of strategic forest investment projects and promote investment attraction.
  (2) Matters necessary for the target and scope of support under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 51 (Lease and use of State or public property) (1) Where deemed necessary for the designation of a strategic zone and the smooth implementation of a strategic forest investment project, the State, etc. may lease, allow the use of, permit the profiting from, or sell the State or public property to a project operator under Article 45(1)1 through 3 through a negotiated contract, notwithstanding the State Property Act or the Public Property and Commodity Management Act.
  (2) Details and conditions of the lease, use, profiting, or sale of State or public property under paragraph (1) shall be governed by the State Property Act or the Public Property and Utensils Management Act.

Article 52 (Support for investment companies) (1) The Mayor/Do Governor shall prepare and implement policies concerning support for investor companies in order to promote attraction of investments into the strategic zones.
  (2) The State may provide assistance necessary for implementing policies referred to in paragraph (1).
  (3) The types, subjects, and standards of policies for supporting investment companies under paragraph (1) and other matters necessary for supporting investment companies shall be prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of City/Do.

Article 53 (Financial assistance for tenant companies in strategic zones) The State, etc. may provide maximum assistance where a project implementor or a tenant company within a strategic zone requests loans for the purchase of land, reduction or exemption of rents for land, etc. or other financial assistance for development projects.

Article 54 (Special cases concerning support for enterprises in strategic zones) (1) The authority of the Minister of SMEs and Startups or the head of a relevant central administrative agency under Article 13(2) of the Act on Facilitation of Purchase of Small and Medium Enterprise-Manufactured Products and Support for Development of Their Markets (limited to the authority to request preferential purchase measures), and the authority of the Minister of SMEs and Startups under Article 26(1) through (3) of that Act, with respect to tenant enterprises in a strategic zone, shall be vested in the Mayor/Do Governor.
  (2) Where a project implementer concludes the contracts for construction works, goods, services, etc. prescribed by Presidential Decree, he or she may grant preferential treatment, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to persons whose principal place of business is located within the jurisdiction of, or adjacent to, the Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the strategic zone.

Article 55 (Expropriation or use of land) (1) Where necessary for implementation of a strategic forest investment project, a project implementer may expropriate or use land, goods, or rights (hereinafter referred to as "land, etc.") under Article 3 of the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects.
  (2) Where an approval and public notice of an implementation plan under Article 47 have been granted and made, project recognition and public notice thereof under Article 20(1) and Article 22 of the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects shall be deemed to have been granted and made, and notwithstanding Article 23(1) and Article 28(1) of that Act, an application for adjudication may be filed within the project period specified in the implementation plan.
  (3) Except as provided in this Act, the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects shall apply mutatis mutandis to expropriation or use of land, etc.

Article 56 (Special cases concerning application of the Mountainous Districts Management Act in strategic zones) (1) Notwithstanding Article 8 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act, where a strategic zone is designated or zones, etc. are designated or determined within a strategic zone, the classification and area of mountainous districts, excluding national forests under the jurisdiction of the Korea Forest Service, may be otherwise prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Notwithstanding Articles 12 and 18 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act, restrictions on acts within the conservation mountainous districts and standards for permission for the conversion of mountainous districts concerning slope and elevation may be otherwise prescribed by Presidential Decree with respect to the strategic forest investment projects.
  (3) Notwithstanding Article 11(1)1 of the Forest Protection Act, where land within a forest protection zone inside a strategic zone is to be used for any of the following facilities, the Mayor/Do Governor may release all or part of such designation:
  1. Facilities necessary for natural recreation forests provided in Article 14 of the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act;
  2. Facilities necessary for forest bathing areas, healing forests, forest camping grounds, and forest leisure sports facilities under Article 20 of the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act;
  3. Facilities necessary for arboretums under Article 7 of the Act on the Creation and Furtherance of Arboretums Act.
  (4) Forest protection zones that may be released under paragraph (3) shall be limited to forests other than national forests under the jurisdiction of the Korea Forest Service and shall be limited to living environment protection zones, landscape protection zones, water resource conservation zones, and disaster prevention protection zones under Article 7(1)1 through 4 of the Forest Protection Act.

Article 57 (Special cases concerning application of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act within strategic zones) The Mayor/Do Governor may determine and implement relaxed standards for the following matters within the scope prescribed by Presidential Decree for the creation of a strategic zone; in such cases, they shall undergo deliberation by the Local Urban Planning Committee established in the City/Do under Article 113 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act:
  1. Restrictions on building construction in the use districts and the specific-use district pursuant to Article 76 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  2. Restrictions on the building-to-land ratio under Article 77 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  3. Restrictions on the floor area ratio according to Article 78 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act.

Article 58 (Special cases concerning deliberation by the Regional Mountainous Districts Management Committee) Notwithstanding Article 22(1)1 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act, the following matters may be deliberated by the Regional Mountainous District Management Committee pursuant to Article 22(2) of the Mountainous Districts Management Act for the purpose of processing affairs delegated to, or falling under the authority of, the Mayor/Do Governor pursuant to Articles 32 and 56:
  1. Modification or release of designation of conservation mountainous districts under Article 6(4) of the Mountainous Districts Management Act;
  2. Permission for temporary use of mountainous districts involving conservation mountainous districts of at least 500,000 square meters under Article 15-2(1) of the Mountainous Districts Management Act;
  3. Release of designation of a forest protection zone under Article 11 of the Forest Protection Act.

Article 59 (Special cases concerning application of the Mountainous Districts Management Act) The authority of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service under Article 17(1) and (2) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 20(1), Article 21(1) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 37(1) through (4) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 38 (1) through (4) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 39(2) and (3) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 40(1) through (3) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 41 (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 42(1) and (2) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 43(1) and (2) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 44(1) and (2) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 47(1) (limited to matters concerning delegated authority), Article 48(1) through (3) (limited to compensation for losses arising from acts concerning delegated authority), Article 49 (limited to hearings concerning delegated authority), Article 50 (limited to fees under delegated authority), and Article 57(3) (limited to imposition and collection of administrative fines under delegated authority) of the Mountainous Districts Management Act shall be delegated to the Mayor/Do Governor.

Article 60 (Special cases concerning application of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act) (1) Notwithstanding Article 29 of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act, where the head of an administrative agency receives a request for consultation on an environmental impact assessment report with respect to a development project under a strategic zone development plan, he or she shall notify the Mayor/Do Governor of the details of consultation within 45 days from the date of receipt of the environmental impact assessment report; if no notification is made within such period, consultation shall be deemed completed.
  (2) Where the head of an administrative agency receives a request for consultation under paragraph (1), and grounds exist for supplementation or adjustment of the environmental impact assessment report under Article 28(3) of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act, he or she may request, on one occasion only, the Mayor/Do Governor or the project implementer to supplement the relevant documents; the period required for such supplementation shall not be included in the consultation period.

Article 61 (Duration of special provisions) (1) The special provisions under Articles 54 through 60 shall remain in effect until the date 5 years after the enforcement of this Act, and if necessary, such period may be extended within 3 years upon deliberation by the State Council.
  (2) The head of a relevant administrative agency shall evaluate the operational performance, etc. of the special cases under Articles 54 through 60 no later than 3 months before the expiration of the duration under paragraph (1) and notify the Mayor/Do Governor of their opinion regarding the extension or abolition of the duration, and the Mayor/Do Governor shall take necessary measures in accordance with the notified opinions.
  (3) The head of a relevant administrative agency may request the competent Mayor/Do Governor to submit data necessary for evaluation under paragraph (2). In such cases, the competent Mayor/Do Governor shall comply with such request, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (4) Matters necessary concerning the method and procedures for evaluation under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER Ⅵ  SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 62 (Protection of rights) Rights arising under this Act shall not be transferred, seized, or offered as security.

Article 63 (Exercise of right of recourse by the State) (1) Where the State, etc. mobilizes personnel, equipment, etc. to prevent further damage caused by the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan, it may advance the expenses required therefor.
  (2) The State, etc. may exercise the right of recourse against a person who caused the mega-wildfirs in Gyeongbuk, Gyeongnam, and Ulsan for expenses incurred during the recovery process.

Article 64 (Delegation and entrustment of authority) The authority of the State or local governments under this Act may, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, be partially delegated or entrusted to the affiliated agencies of central administrative agencies, heads of local governments, or relevant institutions or organizations.

Article 65 (Duty of confidentiality) The current or previous members or employees of the Committee, experts or private organizations and related persons that have performed duties of the Committee shall not provide or divulge information, documents, data, or articles classified as confidential to others, nor use them for any purpose other than performance of the Committee's duties.

Article 66 (Prohibition of impersonation of qualification) (1) No person shall exercise the authority of the Committee by falsely representing themselves as a member or employee of the Committee.
  (2) No person shall organize an organization, engage in collective action, or conduct individual activities for profit under the pretext of supporting victims.

Article 67 (Legal fiction as public officials for purposes of applying penalty provisions) The members of the Committee shall be deemed to be public officials for purposes of applying Articles 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act.

CHAPTER VII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 68 (Penalty provisions) (1) Any person who receives grants, subsidies, or other assistance under this Act by fraud or other improper means shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 5 years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won.
  (2) A person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by imprisonment with prison labor for not more than 1 year, or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won:
  1. A person who has violated Article 65 and failed to comply with the duty of confidentiality;
  2. A person who violates Article 66(1) by falsely representing himself or herself as a member or employee of the Committee;
  3. A person who organizes a group or engages in collective action or individual activities in violation of Article 66(2).

